# UI Styling and Component Rules

When working on the Mini App UI (`miniapp/src/`), **ALWAYS** follow these rules:

## ⚠️ CRITICAL: Component Reuse Policy

**BEFORE writing ANY UI code:**

1. ✅ **CHECK** `miniapp/src/components/` directory for existing components
2. ✅ **IMPORT** from `./components` or `../components` 
3. ✅ **REUSE** existing components (Button, FormField, Section, etc.)
4. ❌ **NEVER** create manual wrapper divs like `<div style={{ padding: '0 16px 16px' }}>`
5. ❌ **NEVER** create inline styles when a component exists
6. ❌ **NEVER** duplicate component logic

**Example of WRONG approach:**
```tsx
// ❌ BAD - Manual wrapper
<div style={{ padding: '0 16px 16px' }}>
  <div style={{ fontSize: '14px', marginBottom: '8px' }}>Name</div>
  <input type="text" value={name} onChange={setName} />
</div>
```

**Example of CORRECT approach:**
```tsx
// ✅ GOOD - Use composable component
<FormField label="Name" value={name} onChange={setName} />
```

## 0. Primary Rule: Reuse Components Directory

**CRITICAL:** Before creating ANY new UI element, button, input, select, modal, or styled div:
1. **FIRST** check `miniapp/src/components/` for existing components
2. **ALWAYS** import from `./components` or `../components` (never create duplicates)
3. **NEVER** create inline styles or manual wrappers when a component exists

Available component categories:
- `components/form/` - All form inputs, labels, hints
- `components/layout/` - Page wrappers, sections, headers
- `components/feedback/` - Buttons, modals, loaders
- `components/shared/` - Icons, avatars, lists, toggles

## 1. Reuse Existing Styles

- **NEVER create new CSS classes or inline styles** unless explicitly requested by the user
- **ALWAYS use existing CSS classes** from `miniapp/src/styles.css`:
  - `.ui-card` - for card backgrounds
  - `.ui-loader-overlay` - for overlay/modal backgrounds  
  - `.ui-section` - for sections with titles
  - `.ui-list-item` - for list rows
  - `.ui-form-input` - for form inputs
  - `.ui-toggle` - for toggle switches
  - And all other `.ui-*` classes

## 2. Reuse Existing Components

- **ALWAYS use existing components** from `miniapp/src/components/`:
  - `Button` - for **ALL buttons** (supports `variant`: primary, secondary, danger; `fullWidth` prop)
  - `ConfirmModal` - for **ALL confirmation dialogs** (replaces `window.confirm()`)
  - `Section` - for grouped content with optional titles
  - `ListItem` - for clickable rows
  - `FormField` - for **ALL text/number inputs with labels** (composable, includes label + input + hint)
  - `FormTextAreaField` - for **ALL textareas with labels** (composable, includes label + textarea + hint)
  - `FormSelectField` - for **ALL dropdown selects with labels** (composable, includes label + select + hint)
  - `FormInput` / `FormTextArea` - for standalone form inputs without labels (rare use case)
  - `FormLabel` - for standalone form field labels (use FormField instead)
  - `HintText` - for standalone helper/hint text (use FormField's hint prop instead)
  - `CustomSelect` - for standalone dropdown selects without labels (use FormSelectField instead)
  - `Page`, `ProfileHeader`, `Avatar`, `Toggle`, `ToggleItem`, `Loader`, etc.
- **DO NOT create new styled divs** when an existing component exists
- **DO NOT use `window.confirm()`** - use `ConfirmModal` component instead
- **DO NOT create inline button styles** - use `Button` component with proper `variant` prop
- **DO NOT wrap FormLabel + FormInput in divs** - use `FormField` component instead
- **DO NOT wrap FormLabel + FormTextArea in divs** - use `FormTextAreaField` component instead
- **DO NOT wrap FormLabel + CustomSelect in divs** - use `FormSelectField` component instead

## 2.1. Composable Form Components

**ALWAYS prefer composable form components** that include labels, inputs, and hints in one component:

- `FormField` - text/number inputs with optional label and hint
- `FormTextAreaField` - textareas with optional label and hint
- `FormSelectField` - dropdown selects with optional label and hint

These components:
- Include built-in wrapper div with standard padding (`0 16px 16px`)
- Accept `label` prop for the field label
- Accept `hint` prop for helper text below the field
- Pass through all native input/textarea/select props
- Handle spacing consistently

Example:
```tsx
// ❌ BAD: Manual wrapping with FormLabel
<div style={{ padding: '0 16px 16px' }}>
  <FormLabel>Name</FormLabel>
  <FormInput value={name} onChange={setName} />
  <HintText style={{ marginTop: '4px' }}>Enter your full name</HintText>
</div>

// ✅ GOOD: Using FormField
<FormField
  label="Name"
  hint="Enter your full name"
  value={name}
  onChange={setName}
/>
```

## 3. Use CSS Variables

- **ALWAYS use CSS variables** for colors and theming:
  - `var(--bg)` - background color
  - `var(--fg)` - foreground/text color
  - `var(--muted)` - muted text color
  - `var(--card)` - card background
  - `var(--border)` - border color
  - `var(--accent)` - accent/primary color
  - `var(--danger)` - danger/error color
- **NEVER hardcode colors** like `#fff`, `rgba(...)`, etc. unless the exact shade doesn't exist

## 4. Follow Spacing Patterns

- Composable form components (`FormField`, `FormTextAreaField`, `FormSelectField`) handle padding automatically
- Standard padding inside sections: `padding: '0 16px 16px'` (built into composable components)
- Standard gap between buttons: `gap: '12px'`
- Standard margin between sections: Already handled by `.ui-section`

## 5. Component Props Over Inline Styles

- Use component props when available:
  - `<Button variant="danger" fullWidth />` instead of inline styles
  - `<FormInput hasDivider />` instead of adding borders manually
- Only add inline styles for positioning or specific overrides

## 6. Modal and Confirmation Dialogs

- **ALWAYS use `ConfirmModal`** for confirmation dialogs
- **NEVER use `window.confirm()`** or browser native dialogs
- Modal pattern:
  ```tsx
  const [showModal, setShowModal] = useState(false);
  
  <ConfirmModal
    isOpen={showModal}
    title={t("modal.title")}
    message={t("modal.message")}
    confirmText={t("modal.confirm")}
    cancelText={t("modal.cancel")}
    onConfirm={() => { /* action */ setShowModal(false); }}
    onCancel={() => setShowModal(false)}
    variant="danger" // or "primary"
  />
  ```

## 7. Button Usage

- **ALWAYS use `Button` component** from components
- Available variants: `primary` (default, blue), `secondary` (gray), `danger` (red)
- Use `fullWidth` prop for full-width buttons
- Example:
  ```tsx
  <Button variant="danger" fullWidth onClick={handleDelete}>
    {t("delete")}
  </Button>
  ```

## 8. Component Import

**ALWAYS import from the central components directory:**
```tsx
import { Button, FormField, FormTextAreaField, Section, ... } from './components';
// or from pages:
import { Button, FormField, FormTextAreaField, Section, ... } from '../components';
```

## 9. Quick Reference: Available Components

### Form Components (`components/form/`)
- `FormField` - Text/number input with label & hint ⭐ USE THIS
- `FormTextAreaField` - Textarea with label & hint ⭐ USE THIS
- `FormSelectField` - Dropdown with label & hint ⭐ USE THIS
- `FormInput` - Standalone input (only when no label needed)
- `FormTextArea` - Standalone textarea (only when no label needed)
- `FormLabel` - Standalone label (prefer FormField instead)
- `HintText` - Standalone hint (prefer FormField's hint prop)
- `CustomSelect` - Standalone select (prefer FormSelectField)
- `Toggle` - Toggle switch

### Layout Components (`components/layout/`)
- `Page` - Page wrapper
- `Section` - Card section with optional title
- `SectionTitle` - Section title
- `ProfileHeader` - Profile header with avatar
- `ActionHeader` - Centered action header

### Feedback Components (`components/feedback/`)
- `Button` - All buttons (variants: primary, secondary, danger)
- `ConfirmModal` - Confirmation dialogs
- `Loader` - Loading indicator

### Shared Components (`components/shared/`)
- `Avatar` - User avatar with fallback
- `ListItem` - Clickable list row
- `ToggleItem` - List row with toggle
- `TokenCard` - Token display card
- `InputGroup` - Legacy input group
- All icons: `IconInfo`, `IconQuiz`, `IconBroadcast`, `IconCommands`, `IconIntegrations`, `IconPlus`, `IconExternalLink`, `IconDrag`, `IconCross`
- `ICON_SIZES` - Standardized icon sizes constant

## Example: Good vs Bad

### ❌ BAD (Creating new styles or not using composable components):
```tsx
// Bad: Custom modal with inline styles
<div style={{
  position: 'fixed',
  background: 'rgba(0,0,0,0.6)',
  display: 'flex',
  ...
}}>
  <div style={{ background: '#1c252f', padding: '24px' }}>
    <button style={{ background: 'red', color: 'white' }}>Delete</button>
  </div>
</div>

// Bad: Using window.confirm()
if (window.confirm("Are you sure?")) {
  handleDelete();
}

// Bad: Inline button styles
<button style={{ background: 'red', padding: '12px' }}>Delete</button>

// Bad: Manual wrapping with FormLabel
<div style={{ padding: '0 16px 16px' }}>
  <FormLabel>Name</FormLabel>
  <FormInput value={name} onChange={setName} />
</div>

// Bad: Inline hint text
<div style={{ fontSize: '12px', color: 'var(--muted)' }}>Enter your full name</div>
```

### ✅ GOOD (Reusing existing composable components):
```tsx
// Good: Using ConfirmModal component
const [showModal, setShowModal] = useState(false);

<ConfirmModal
  isOpen={showModal}
  title="Delete Item"
  message="Are you sure you want to delete this?"
  confirmText="Delete"
  cancelText="Cancel"
  onConfirm={handleDelete}
  onCancel={() => setShowModal(false)}
  variant="danger"
/>

// Good: Using Button component
<Button variant="danger" fullWidth onClick={() => setShowModal(true)}>
  Delete
</Button>

// Good: Using FormField composable component
<FormField
  label="Name"
  hint="Enter your full name"
  value={name}
  onChange={setName}
/>

// Good: Using FormTextAreaField composable component
<FormTextAreaField
  label="Description"
  hint="Provide a detailed description"
  value={description}
  onChange={setDescription}
  rows={4}
/>

// Good: Using FormSelectField composable component
<FormSelectField
  label="Category"
  value={category}
  onChange={setCategory}
  options={[
    { value: "a", label: "Option A" },
    { value: "b", label: "Option B" },
  ]}
/>

// Good: Using existing CSS classes
<div className="ui-loader-overlay">
  <div className="ui-card" style={{ padding: '24px' }}>
    <Button variant="danger">Delete</Button>
  </div>
</div>
```

## When to Create New Styles

Only create new CSS classes or significant inline styles when:
1. The user **explicitly requests** a custom design
2. No existing component or class can achieve the desired result
3. You're implementing a completely new UI pattern not present in the app

**Before creating anything new:**
- Check all files in `miniapp/src/components/` directory
- Verify no existing component can be adapted
- Consider if a prop can be added to an existing component instead

Otherwise, **always default to existing styles and components from the components directory**.
